          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "**Urgency:** The check found no emergency pattern in what you described, so this is routine. That is not a clearance. If your symptoms become severe, worsening, or new, seek medical advice.\n\n**Is three in a year a concern?** By the usual definition, yes. The Infectious Diseases and Urology Society guidance (*Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*, cited as guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment) defines recurrence as two or more infections in six months, or three or more in a year. That guidance covers uncomplicated infection: a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function. If any of that doesn't describe you, a different approach applies. The sources I could retrieve don't say whether three infections should prompt further investigation or referral, so ask your clinician. The guidance also says urine culture is useful when infection recurs, so a clinician may want a culture before treating.\n\n**Preventive options the guidance describes:** behaviour changes; vaginal oestrogen after menopause; and antibiotic prophylaxis, either continuous or taken after intercourse, for frequent and disabling recurrence, weighed against the risk of antibiotic resistance. Cranberry products and extra fluids are described as having limited and mixed evidence. The sources don't say how strong the evidence is for behaviour changes or prophylaxis, so I can't grade them.\n\n**Where guidance differs:** Some European guidance supports offering symptom relief with delayed or no antibiotics for mild cystitis, as a shared decision. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default, because of symptom duration and the risk of kidney involvement. This is a disagreement about how much antibiotic exposure to avoid, not about the underlying facts.\n\n**Red flags that need prompt assessment, not a routine appointment** (General clinical reference, Urinary tract infection):\n- Fever with flank or back pain\n- Shaking chills\n- Vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down\n- Confusion\n- Any symptoms during pregnancy\n- Symptoms if you have one kidney or a transplant\n- Symptoms that get worse despite 48 hours of treatment\n\nBlood in the urine was not named as a red flag in the passages I retrieved, so do not treat this list as complete. If you notice any of these signs, contact a clinician or urgent care without waiting.\n\n**Seek emergency care immediately** if fever, a fast heartbeat, low blood pressure and confusion occur together. This pattern can indicate sepsis, a serious response to infection. Kidney infection is more serious than bladder infection, and the guidance notes an infection can become dangerous even when it started minor.\n\n**To discuss with a clinician:** whether your infections count as uncomplicated, whether further tests are worth considering, and which prevention options suit you. The guidance also advises against treating bacteria found in urine without symptoms, outside pregnancy or before certain procedures.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
